From 94b8f6cbd9250478f62d47d322e0659d0e2c62fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chen Jun <newdays-cj@live.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 12:22:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH v2 8/8] mm/hugetlb: retire boot metadata budget as pages are
 prepared

Prepare each candidate mapping before publishing its reservation. Retire backing credit only on success and account for physically allocated page tables on both success and failure. Require ordinary fallbacks to fit the remaining metadata and normal-memory reserves, otherwise withdraw the unpublished candidate.

Prefer metadata fragments while preserving mirrored-memory allocation policy. Treat an exhausted prospective budget as a short allocation. Document the high-occupancy and oversized-request regressions.

Signed-off-by: Chen Jun <newdays-cj@live.com>
Co-authored-by: sisyphus-dev-ai <sisyphus-dev-ai@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst | 52 +++++++++--
 mm/hugetlb.c                                 | 85 +++++++++++++----
 mm/hugetlb_vmemmap.h                         |  2 +-
 mm/sparse-vmemmap.c                          | 96 +++++++++++++++++---
 4 files changed, 196 insertions(+), 39 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst b/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst
index 6ffb804ed128..5b954542e65e 100644
--- a/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst
+++ b/Documentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst
@@ -496,12 +496,17 @@ If no suitable range exists, the actual pool may be smaller than requested;
 a cross-zone range is not added to the pool.
 
 Only the required vmemmap backing is allocated for an optimized page; the
-remaining descriptors share it. The path reserves a conservative budget for
-subsequent ordinary metadata and page tables, including when optimization
-must fall back. ``hugepage_prohibit_sz=`` continues to protect the requested
-ordinary-memory reserve. Tail mappings become read-only after descriptor
-initialization, and shrinking or demoting the pool uses the existing
-vmemmap restoration path.
+remaining descriptors share it. The path initially budgets for ordinary
+metadata and page tables, then prepares each candidate's shared mappings
+before publishing the reservation. A successful preparation retires that
+candidate's backing requirement and the tables already physically allocated
+from the future budget. Failed preparation earns no backing saving: the
+candidate remains ordinary only if its full metadata budget and the
+``hugepage_prohibit_sz=`` reserve still fit; otherwise its reservation is
+withdrawn. The ordinary-memory reserve is not spent as optimization credit.
+Tail mappings become read-only after descriptor initialization, with the
+per-page HVO protection already active. Shrinking or demoting the pool uses
+the existing vmemmap restoration path.
 
 An early diagnostic reports how many pages were optimized and how much
 redundant vmemmap backing was not allocated. The reported saving excludes
@@ -558,6 +563,10 @@ Run one VM at a time, with at least 3 GiB available to QEMU::
     NODES=2 MEMORY=6144 EXPECTED_POOL=2 REQUESTED_POOL=0:1,1:1 \
         sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
         "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" numa.log
+    MEMORY=2112 REQUESTED_POOL=2 EXPECTED_POOL=1 \
+        EXTRA_CMDLINE='cma=0 memmap=exactmap,256M@0x40000000,1G@0x80000000,64M@0xc0000000' \
+        sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" oversized-request.log
 
 Each invocation compiles ``init.c`` with ``-static -Wall -Wextra -Werror``,
 creates an uncompressed initramfs using ``mktemp`` and ``gen_init_cpio``,
@@ -584,6 +593,37 @@ guest kernel. The guest checks that both node pool counters are available
 and that their sum equals the expected global count. Arbitrary QEMU
 arguments are not exposed.
 
+``RAM_RESERVE=on|off`` controls the QEMU RAM backend's virtual memory
+reservation and defaults to ``on``, preserving the existing behavior.
+``off`` uses an explicit ``memory-backend-ram`` object for a single node;
+with two nodes the setting applies to both backend objects. This permits
+a large guest address space on a host whose commit limit would otherwise
+reject it. It does not provide 128 GiB of resident host RAM: pages actually
+touched by the guest still consume host memory. Run one VM at a time and
+allow for the guest's resident working set.
+
+For the high-RAM pool regression, use a kernel with deferred struct-page
+initialization and boot-time vmemmap optimization enabled, and disable CMA
+for this isolated case::
+
+    RAM_RESERVE=off MEMORY=131072 EXPECTED_POOL=126 EXTRA_CMDLINE='cma=0' \
+        sh "$testdir/run.sh" "$OUT/arch/arm64/boot/Image" \
+        "$OUT/usr/gen_init_cpio" high-pool.log
+
+This requests and expects 126 1 GiB pages in a 128 GiB guest virtual memory
+configuration. The known baseline at ``dc55ffbf8c05`` allocates only 125
+pages and fails the initial pool assertion. Keep the expected count at 126
+and retain all lifecycle checks when comparing a candidate: honoring the
+pool request without usable mappings or correct release is not a pass.
+This recipe does not measure whole-system peak memory use.
+
+The oversized-request recipe uses the existing ``memmap=`` controls to
+provide a small kernel bank, one aligned 1 GiB bank, and a small metadata
+bank. Only one huge page can be reserved. Requesting two must report the
+short allocation and complete the one-page lifecycle without a kernel BUG;
+the prospective metadata credit is not evidence that another candidate
+exists.
+
 ``EXPECTED_POOL`` defaults to 1 and sets the guest's expected count.
 ``REQUESTED_POOL`` defaults to that count and sets the boot request; it also
 accepts the kernel's node-specific count syntax. Separate counts allow tests
